{"_comment": "Persons obtaining lawful permanent residence counted by COUNTRY OF LAST RESIDENCE against the same people counted by COUNTRY OF BIRTH. A positive gap means more people arrived FROM a country than were BORN in it -- the country served as a waypoint. THE TWO TABLES ARE SEPARATE TOTALS, NOT A CROSS-TABULATION: no individual is traced from a birthplace to a residence, so a surplus in one country CANNOT BE ATTRIBUTED to any particular origin however well the deficits elsewhere seem to match. THIS DOES NOT REACH TEMPORARY VISAS. Every nonimmigrant series -- TN, H-1B, F-1, L-1 -- is published by CITIZENSHIP ONLY; country of birth is not published for any of them, so a naturalised citizen cannot be distinguished from a native-born one anywhere in the visa data. Who holds a given nonimmigrant status by origin has no published answer. Countries below 3,000 are excluded because the gap is then noise.", "source": "DHS Yearbook of Immigration Statistics, tables 2 and 3", "source_url": "https://ohss.dhs.gov/topics/immigration/yearbook", "years": ["2014", "2015", "2016", "2017", "2018", "2019", "2020", "2021", "2022", "2023"], "latest_year": "2023", "floor": 3000, "countries": 63, "waypoints": [{"country": "Canada", "residence": 18590, "birth": 11870, "gap": 6720, "gap_pct_of_residence": 36.1, "series": {"2014": {"residence": 17670, "birth": 11590, "gap": 6080, "gap_pct_of_residence": 34.4}, "2015": {"residence": 19310, "birth": 12670, "gap": 6640, "gap_pct_of_residence": 34.4}, "2016": {"residence": 19350, "birth": 12790, "gap": 6560, "gap_pct_of_residence": 33.9}, "2017": {"residence": 18470, "birth": 11480, "gap": 6990, "gap_pct_of_residence": 37.8}, "2018": {"residence": 14340, "birth": 9900, "gap": 4440, "gap_pct_of_residence": 31.0}, "2019": {"residence": 14720, "birth": 11390, "gap": 3330, "gap_pct_of_residence": 22.6}, "2020": {"residence": 11300, "birth": 9530, "gap": 1770, "gap_pct_of_residence": 15.7}, "2021": {"residence": 12050, "birth": 9980, "gap": 207